              "HOW_TO_READ_LOGS.txt": {
                "type": "file",
                "permissions": "644",
                "owner": "root",
                "content": "================================================================================\n          APOLLO SECOPS FIELD MANUAL // HOW TO READ SYSTEM LOGS\n================================================================================\nAlice \u2014 when an attack happens, system daemons log events to /var/log/.\nRaw logs look dense, but every line follows a strict 4-part anatomy:\n\n  [ TIMESTAMP ]  [ HOST ]  [ DAEMON/SERVICE ]   [ EVENT MESSAGE ]\n  03:38:10       apollo    sshd[204]:           Invalid user operator from 192.168.1.105\n  \u2514\u2500\u2500 When       \u2514\u2500\u2500 Where \u2514\u2500\u2500 Who logged it    \u2514\u2500\u2500 What actually happened\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1. THE 4 COMMON INCIDENT DAEMONS:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u2022 'sshd[PID]'   : Secure Shell logins.\n                  - \"Failed password\" = Unauthorized login attempt (brute force).\n                  - \"Accepted password\" = Successful breach / intruder entry.\n\n\u2022 'sudo'        : Administrator privilege escalation.\n                  - \"COMMAND=...\" = Exact command executed with root powers.\n\n\u2022 'kernel'      : Core OS hardware & network events.\n                  - \"Interface link state degraded: DOWN\" = Network port disabled.\n\n\u2022 'systemd[1]'  : System service supervisor.\n                  - \"code=killed, status=9/KILL\" = Service forcefully terminated.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2. YOUR LOG INVESTIGATION TOOLKIT:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\u2022 cat <file>            : Stream an entire log from start to finish.\n\u2022 head -n 5 <file>      : Peek at the earliest events (e.g. system boot).\n\u2022 tail -n 10 <file>     : Focus on the latest events (e.g. recent attack activity).\n\u2022 grep -i \"pattern\" <file>: Filter the noise to find specific keywords or error tags.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3. THE INVESTIGATOR'S MINDSET:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhen triaging a compromised machine, ask yourself three core questions:\n  1. Entry Point  : How did the intruder get in, and what commands did they run?\n  2. Persistence  : Did they spawn any hidden background processes or alter files?\n  3. Sabotage     : What network ports or critical services did they take down?\n\nUse your filtering tools to investigate the logs and uncover the trail.\n================================================================================\n"
